Raw dev notes -- The Unsung Heroes of RAG: Syncs, Fixes, and Indexing

Okay, so this past week has been a deep dive into the guts of the RAG Knowledge Base Pipeline. The whole point of this project is to keep our LLM-powered assistant smart and up-to-date by feeding it fresh, relevant info. It's all about making sure the assistant has the right answers and can point to where they came from.

What I've been up to lately feels like a mix of plumbing, detective work, and library organization. The biggest chunk of work, judging by the commit log, was just keeping the data flowing. There were a ton of `sync` commits, pulling new Q&A pairs from our main knowledge source. It's like a daily harvest, making sure the LLM's brain is constantly refreshed.

Beyond the routine syncing, I added a couple of new email watchers. One for `***-py` updates – gotta stay on top of changes to that service – and another for `WhatsApp number health`. That second one immediately led to a classic "why isn't this working?!" moment. Turns out, the watcher was getting 403 Forbidden errors because I forgot to set a `User-Agent` header. Man, those little web request details always bite you when you least expect it. It's such a small fix, but it can be a real head-scratcher until you remember the basics.

Another fun little deployment hiccup involved the `daily_deploy` script. It was aborting `git pull` operations because of some local `sync-state drift`. Basically, my automated deployment was getting confused by its own local changes. The fix was to explicitly tell git to checkout `origin/main` to `HEAD`, which basically forces it to align with the remote. It's a small detail, but crucial for robust automation. Nothing worse than your daily deployment failing silently.

The more substantial work was around the "Source-resolution index builder." This is a pretty critical piece for RAG, as it helps us map raw text passages back to their original source, complete with type, title, and a URL if available. This is how the LLM can say "I got this from X article" and actually link to it. I also built in an incremental shortlink cache, which should make things faster and more consistent. And, of course, I hooked that into the `daily_deploy` process so the cache gets refreshed regularly.

Honestly, this past week felt like a lot of "invisible" work. Fixing a `User-Agent` header, wrestling with `git pull` in an automated script, building an internal index – none of this is flashy, but it's absolutely foundational. It's frustrating when you hit those small, finicky bugs, but there's a real satisfaction in squashing them and making the whole system more robust. It's a constant reminder that building reliable software isn't just about new features, but about meticulous attention to detail and anticipating all the ways things can go wrong. I'm learning a lot about building resilient pipelines and the importance of good monitoring.

Next up: Start integrating this improved source resolution into the actual RAG query process.
